AB  - Triage in emergency departments (EDs) is fundamental in prioritizing patient care and optimizing the allocation of limited healthcare resources. However, conventional triage systems remain susceptible to inconsistencies such as undertriage and overtriage, particularly in high-demand clinical environments. This study presents a machine learning-based approach for predicting triage levels using structured data from the emergency unit of a high-complexity regional hospital in Chile. The dataset was filtered to include only adult and dental consultation records, excluding pediatric and obstetric-gynecological cases due to their different clinical characteristics. Patients were categorized into five triage levels (C1 to C5), revealing a strong class imbalance, with C2 and C3 comprising nearly 90% of all records. The study implemented three tree-based gradient boosting algorithms, XGBoost, CatBoost, and LightGBM, in conjunction with Bayesian hyperparameter optimization and SVMSMOTE to mitigate class imbalance. Data preprocessing involved Random Forest-based imputation, standardization of numerical features, and one-hot encoding of categorical variables. The results indicate that XGBoost outperformed CatBoost and LightGBM in terms of overall accuracy (0.73) and weighted F1-score (0.72), particularly in the most frequent categories (C2 and C3). CatBoost, while exhibiting slightly lower global accuracy, demonstrated greater sensitivity in the minority class C1. However, all three models showed limitations in predicting the C5 category. The main contribution of this work is to demonstrate the feasibility and limitations of multiclass (C1–C5) triage prediction using gradient boosting models with Bayesian optimization and SHAP interpretability under extreme class imbalance.
