AB  - Emergency departments operate under high patient volume and clinical uncertainty, where early identification of patients at risk of in-hospital mortality remains a major clinical challenge. Traditional systems rely on clinician judgment and rule-based scoring, which can be subjective and slow for highacuity patients. We propose an explainable machine learning framework for early prediction of in-hospital mortality using only information available at arrival, thereby aligning prediction with real-world early assessment constraints and avoiding reliance on retrospectively available hospital data. The approach is evaluated on the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care and its emergency department subset, combining structured demographics, physiological data, and unstructured chief complaints. Ensemble models achieved strong predictive performance while remaining interpretable via SHapley Additive exPlanations, highlighting age, initial vital signs, prior emergency visits, and arrival mode as key risk factors. These results demonstrate the potential of explainable artificial intelligence to support early risk assessment and improve the identification of emergency department patients at high risk of in-hospital mortality.
