AB  - Over the last decade, the adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) within mental healthcare has expanded rapidly, emerging as a potential response to both the increasing demand for psychiatric care and the shortage of qualified professionals. This review synthesises based on recent evidence (2019–2025) exploring applications of AI in mental health care, concentrating on screening, diagnosis, intervention delivery, monitoring, and explainable AI (XAI) practices. Comprehensive coverage was pursued by applying a systematic and predefined search strategy across relevant sources by using major databases such as IEEE Xplore, PsycINFO, PubMed, Scopus, and Google Scholar, incorporating both clinical evidence and technical contributions. Artificial intelligence methods including natural language processing, acoustic analysis, visual recognition, and multimodal fusion are increasingly utilised for both clinical applications and preventive mental health interventions. Commercial tools and research prototypes are reviewed within three categories: chatbot-based therapy and triage, emotional well-being applications, and "smart" mental health tools for diagnostics and monitoring. While these technologies show promise in improving access, scalability, and personalisation, persistent challenges include limited long-term evidence, algorithmic bias, privacy concerns, lack of empathy, trustworthiness, and the need for culturally adaptive systems. Explainable-AI (XAI) emerges as a critical enabler for trust, transparency, and safe adoption, especially in high-stakes mental health decisions and support. Future research should prioritise large-scale, longitudinal trials, multimodal integration, and regulatory frameworks that align with rapid technical advances
