AB  - Intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) is an emergent and potentially life-threatening diagnosis caused by intracranial or surrounding structure bleeding. Detection needs to be timely and correct to enhance outcomes in patients but is frequently impeded by radiologist shortages, limited time, and human error in high-pressure or low-resource environments. In order to overcome this challenge, we introduce a real-time, interpretable deep model based on the ResNet-50 architecture for multi-label classification of the five most common forms of brain hemorrhages: subdural, subarachnoid, intraventricular, intraparenchymal, and epidural. The model is trained on a balanced portion of the RSNA Intracranial Hemorrhage Detection dataset and makes use of sigmoid activation with binary cross-entropy loss to manage multiple labels per CT scan. To further the transparency and trustworthiness, we integrate Grad-CAM (Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping), which produces heatmaps demarcating regions of interest within the CT images that impacted the model's predictions. Such visual interpretability assists the clinicians in comprehending and validating the AI's outputs. Moreover, a light-weight Streamlit-based web interface enables users to upload scans and get on-the-fly predictions along with the respective Grad-CAM outputs. The system offers a useful, easy-to-use tool for supporting emergency triage and facilitating the use of explainable AI in radiological workflows.
