AB  - The chest disease is also sensitive and equitable in diagnosis, though it is a great battle in resource-poor practices in which expert radiological interpretation and complete laboratory support are not always available. But here are the most recent events. Variance in artificial intelligence (AI) would help connect different types of data, such as chest radiographs, basic clinical findings, and lab results, into useful automated diagnostic pipelines. This multimodal solution can be more predictive and clinically useful when each source of data utilizes its own advantage. Modern AI-based applications have been very good at making predictions about the severity of an illness, the needed treatments, and the outcome for the patient. and the scores for the area under the receiver operating curve (AUROC). Combining imaging, laboratory, and clinical data with a score of over 0.75. More importantly, the AI algorithms can surpass solo models and are interpretable by attributing features, which contributes to the triage of resources and decision-making by non-specialist practitioners. However, there are still issues concerning algorithmic biases, failure to diagnose underserved subpopulations, and the extrapolation of models on variable patients demographics and institutional settings. Safe and effective deployment must be thoroughly validated in the real-world clinical context, and equity of data represented and clear reports must be considered. The use of multimodal AI solutions has the potential to democratize the access to quality diagnostics of chest diseases in healthcare systems with limited resources, and, finally, health disparities and patient outcomes will be reduced.
