AB  - The given paper presents a new system known as Call2Care to attempt to automate preliminary medical triage through voice and call recordings in real-time. The framework integrates advanced speech recognition, natural language processing to transcribe patient conversations (NLP), and machine learning are used, symptomatic information to be extracted, formatted, and anticipated likely types of disease. Known to be developed to work hard when used in noisy telephonic conditions, the system will overcome issues like fluctuating audio quality, regional accents and non-technical patient vocabulary. We checked our system with fake doctor calls and real voice recordings from people. During the tests, it was able to correctly find the main symptoms and predict the right illness about 86 percent of the time. The paper outlines the system architecture, which puts an increased emphasis on interpretability and privacy, and its potential to provide healthcare access to distant and underserved population as a decision-support tool to the human operators.
