AB  - Tuberculosis (TB) detection from chest radiographs requires models that balance predictive accuracy with transparent decision-making. This study introduces an explainable pipeline combining deep convolutional feature extraction with SHAP-based feature pruning, followed by gradient-boosted classification. Preprocessed lung-masked X-rays from the TBX11K (Simplified) dataset were used, with training and testing performed on a stratified 80/20 split. Global and local SHAP values guided the elimination of non-informative descriptors, while Grad-CAM attention maps were evaluated against ground-truth bounding boxes using Intersection-over-Union (IoU) and Dice metrics to quantify spatial fidelity. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves robust performance (Accuracy: 95.6%, AUROC: 0.969) and consistently surpasses both CNN and CNN+XGBoost baselines. Reliability was further assessed using Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and Brier score, ensuring well-calibrated probability outputs for threshold-sensitive clinical decisions. Confusion matrix analysis highlighted common failure modes, such as subtle apical opacities, while sensitivity checks on pruning thresholds confirmed the model’s robustness. The findings indicate that SHAP-guided feature selection improves both predictive efficiency and interpretability, offering a reproducible and clinically aligned approach for TB triage using chest radiographs.
