AB  - The quick and accurate distinction between intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) and ischemic stroke is very important for emergency neurological triage since these diseases necessitate treatments that are totally opposite from each other. The present study gives a detailed deep learning framework for automated brain CT pathology classification which is tested on three complementary case studies. We performed a thorough comparison of traditional machine learning (XGBoost, Random Forest, SVM, Logistic Regression) versus state-of-the-art deep learning architectures (EfficientNet-B0/B7, ResNet18/50/152, DenseNet121, ConvNeXt-Large, Swin Transformer) using 8,097-13,500 CT images classified into seven hemorrhage categories (Normal, Ischemia, Intraventricular, Intraparenchymal, Subarachnoid, Epidural, Subdural). In Case Study 1, EfficientNet-B0 accomplished seven-class accuracy of $90.33\%}$ with $4.0M$ parameters, surpassing traditional ML by 17.79 percentage points. In Case Study 2, three-window CT preprocessing (Brain/Subdural/Bone windows) was applied with ensemble stacking achieving clinical-grade 97.26% four-class accuracy. In Case Study 3, a near-perfect 99.21% three-class accuracy was reached through Swin Transformer-based voting ensemble. Explainable AI integration (Grad-CAM, Grad-CAM++, LIME) confirmed that the models accurately concentrated on the sites of hemorrhage and ischemia, thus excluding artifacts. The real-time inference $(<20 ~\text{ms}$ per image) makes it suitable for emergency situations. The findings prove that the triaging process can be fully automated for neurological emergencies, which need very quick human-like expert-level decisions.
