AB  - Tuberculosis (TB) remains a major global health threat, necessitating rapid and accurate diagnostic tools. Conventional Chest X-ray (CXR) interpretation is often hampered by resource constraints and human variability. This paper presents DPVM-MS (Dual-Path Vision Model with Mirage Synthesizer), a novel hybrid architecture for pulmonary tuberculosis (PB) detection from chest radiography (CXR) images, that integrates both ResNet50 for local image features and ViT-Base Transformer for the global context in the image via Multihead Attention mechanism. The architecture was deliberately designed to enhance generalization and regularization by introducing the novel Mirage Synthesizer (MS), a learnable and perturbatively designed feature-space augmentation module, along with a stabilizing Progressive Fine-Tuning (PFT) regimen. The evaluation included strict patient-wise stratification and cross-dataset validation across four cohorts: Shenzhen (N=662) and Montgomery (N=138) and DATASET A (N=156) and DATASET B (N=122) (from the National Institute of Tuberculosis and Respiratory Diseases, New Delhi). DPVM-MS achieved high validation accuracies of 90.62% to 96.00% and significantly outperformed the single-path baselines; and for balanced generalization, it achieved perfect Specificity (1.0000) in DATASET B and perfect Sensitivity (1.0000) in the Montgomery dataset, which demonstrated it was a highly accurate and robust positioning it as a resilient diagnostic tool suitable for WHO screening and triage recommendations in low-resource settings.
