AB  - Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is one of the leading causes of global mortality and disability. Early identification and risk prediction of CVD are crucial for its prevention and effective clinical management. This study proposes a CVD risk prediction model based on the Fast-Tunable Optimized Stacked Ensemble Learning (FT-OSEL) architecture. Building upon the original Optimized Stacked Ensemble Learning (OSEL) framework, FT-OSEL integrates lightweight network structures, adaptive fine-tuning mechanisms, parallel training strategies leveraging task-level parallelism, and knowledge distillation techniques, achieving significant improvements in training speed, prediction accuracy, and fine-tunability. The proposed model enables interpretable risk factor analysis and probabilistic predictions through temperature-scaled knowledge distillation, where the original OSEL serves as the teacher model and FT-OSEL as the student model. It further integrates established interpretability techniques—SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME)—to enhance transparency. Cross-dataset validation on an independent external cohort confirms its generalization capability. Experimental results indicate that FT-OSEL not only surpasses conventional approaches but also improves upon the original OSEL architecture, offering a more efficient and interpretable solution for early CVD screening.
