AB  - Pneumonia remains a major global health challenge requiring reliable diagnostic solutions. Current chest X-ray analysis faces limitations due to variability across radiologists and imaging protocols. This study develops a hybrid vision transformer and ResNet architecture with contrastive domain adaptation, combining global context learning and localized feature extraction while improving out-of-sample generalization. A clinical integration module fuses C-reactive protein levels and patient age, improving specificity relative to the image-only model. Validation on 5863 pediatric chest X-rays from Guangzhou Maternal and Child Medical Center achieved an AUC 0.970 (95 \% CI 0.963-0.977) and an accuracy of 91.7\%. Incorporating C-reactive-protein and age features raised specificity from 89% to $93 \%(+4.0 \mathrm{pp})$ over the image-only baseline, while visual explanations reached 89% pixel-level agreement with radiologist annotations. Although the current study relies on a single-center cohort, the proposed framework offers a transparent and readily deployable solution for pneumonia triage in resource-limited settings.
