AB  - COVID-19 disrupted emergency department (ED) arrival patterns, creating challenges for traditional forecasting models used in hospital resource planning. This study analyzes ED arrival data from two Spanish hospitals to assess how the pandemic period affects Machine Learning (ML) model performance and identify optimal forecasting strategies. We applied multiple ML techniques (XGBoost, Random Forest, Elastic Net) and timeseries algorithms (ARIMA, Prophet) using both daily and work shift temporal aggregations, incorporating exogenous calendar variables, including holidays and academic schedules. Models were trained on pandemic-inclusive and post-pandemic periods to evaluate the impact of including disrupted historical data. Results demonstrate that models trained exclusively on postpandemic data consistently outperform those including pandemic data, achieving R2 improvements of up to 0.13 for 4-month forecasts. Work shift aggregation significantly enhanced performance compared to daily aggregation (R2 = 0. 9 2 vs 0. 6 4 for best models). SHAP analysis revealed that recent patient arrivals and calendarrelated features serve as the most important predictors across all scenarios. These findings indicate that excluding pandemic data from training datasets and employing finer temporal aggregation substantially improve ED arrival forecasting accuracy, providing practical benefits for hospital operations and resource allocation.
