AB  - Differentiating between ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke is important, as both are major causes of death and disability. With the development of medical imaging, machine learning (ML) is also applied to stroke detection and triage. This work focuses on recent advances in the machine learning methods used for stroke detection that appeared from 2020 till 2025 in CT/MRI images. In this work, we analyze and compare the evolution of deep-learning-based architectures, such as CNN, U-Net, transformers, and hybrid ensembles, from classical classifiers with handcrafted modalities. This review is based on how to categorize lesion types, localize hemorrhage, and isolate ischemic infarctions, as well as combined imaging strategies. Some recent advances like transfer learning, low-power CNNs for edge applications, uncertainty-aware ideas, and explainable AI for improved clinical interpretation are also discussed. But there still exist many challenges, including domain shifts across scanners, multi-center validation limited in scale, interpretability lacking in a model, and easy association with patient records under the privacy constraint. This review details methodological advancements and unresolved challenges associated with the above-mentioned methodologies. We view the ability to recognize strokes with ML as a key enabler that can help emerging needs like real-time, robust, and equitable diagnostic support systems toward ultimately beginning to deploy AI in acute stroke management.
