AB  - This study presents a reinforcement learning (RL) framework for optimizing intelligent decision-making policies in dynamic, domain-agnostic environments. Unlike traditional rule-based systems, RL agents adapt to uncertainty, learn from interaction, and converge toward efficient strategies over time. We implement and evaluate Deep Q-Network (DQN) and Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) using real-data-inspired benchmarks, measuring performance through cumulative rewards, policy entropy, action distribution, convergence stability, and sample efficiency. The results demonstrate PPO's superior learning stability and faster convergence compared to DQN. Baseline data visualizations, such as triage delay patterns and severity-linked outcomes, provide reference points to highlight how RL outperforms static protocols. These evaluations confirm that RL is wellsuited for scalable, real-time decision automation, offering a reliable alternative where traditional systems are limited by rigidity and inefficiency. The proposed approach sets a foundation for deploying RL in complex, adaptive control scenarios.
