AB  - Effective triage of aortic abnormalities on non-contrast CT (NCCT) is critical for emergency care. Yet automated detection remains difficult due to low vessel–background contrast, resulting in unstable pseudo-segmentations and unreliable supervision. We address this by transforming pseudo-mask instability into a source of structural self-supervision. Instead of suppressing such variability, our framework leverages it to learn normative aortic anatomy and detect deviations indicative of pathology. It integrates three key modules: (1) Prompt-invariant Structural Embedding (PISE) enforces feature consistency across multiple SAM-based prompts; (2) Region-aware Disentanglement (RAD) isolates vessel-wall representations from surrounding tissues; (3) Consistency Evidence Map (CEM) provides transparent, radiologistaligned visualization of abnormal regions. On 363 multicenter NCCT scans, the proposed framework achieved an improvement of 0.056 in the Silhouette score, $\approx 45 \%$ lower representation variance, and AUROC $\approx 0.79$ for aortic dissection triage—outperforming unsupervised baselines and approaching expert-level interpretability. This study establishes a structural self-supervised paradigm that converts foundation-model uncertainty into structure-aware supervision, advancing interpretable, label-efficient, and scalable medical imaging AI for clinical deployment.
