AB  - Online Symptom checkers provide quick, preliminary insights into potential health issues based on user-entered symptoms and data from medical wearable devices. These are gaining popularity worldwide because they empower self-assessment, guide triage decisions, and promote timely case-seeking; however, most existing systems rely on opaque, black-box machine learning or deep learning, or on rigid rule-based approaches, which undermine user trust and transparency. In this paper, we propose an explainable hybrid rule-based and machine learning model for the early detection of chronic diseases. Clinical rules, defined by experts and consisting of IF-THEN elements, are used to identify high-confidence, high-risk symptom patterns. In contrast, machine learning models are applied to the inference of complex or ambiguous instances. Let these symptom-based features and ambiguous instances be merged using hybrid fusion techniques and further classified using a machine learning classifier. The system prioritizes explainability by highlighting activated rules and clinical features for every decision. Two different clinical datasets have been selected for Experimental assessments: the first is the Heart Disease Cleveland dataset, and the second is the Chronic Kidney Disease dataset. These results suggested that the framework has the potential to provide accessible, reliable, symptom-based health advice at the initial stage.
