AB  - Eye diseases such as Diabetic Retinopathy (DR), Glaucoma, and Cataract are among the main causes of global blindness, making early detection crucial to prevent irreversible retinal impairment. However, the manual evaluation of fundus images by ophthalmologists continues to be the primary diagnostic approach, limited by the availability of human resources, particularly in rural or disadvantaged areas. This study introduces an automated multi-class classification system for eye diseases using a deep learning model based on the EfficientNetB3 architecture, supported by Grad-CAM visualization to improve model interpretability. The system was trained using the Ocular Disease Intelligent Recognition (ODIR) dataset, which was categorized into five classes, including Normal, Diabetic Retinopathy (DR), Glaucoma, Cataract, and Other Diseases, the which has been simplified to only contain Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD). Pre-processing includes Circular Border Cropping, RGB Channel Extraction, CLAHE, Median Filtering, resizing to $300 \times 300$ pixels, and data augmentation. The model included pretrained ImageNet weights and was fine-tuned on the last $\mathbf{1 0 0}$ layers with a learning rate of $1 \mathrm{e}-6$ and a dropout rate of 0.3. The proposed model achieved a training accuracy of 97.08%, a validation accuracy of 87.20%, and a testing accuracy of 89.26%. These results indicate significant potential for medical application. The model can assist with triage or function as diagnostic tool in teleophthalmology and radiology processes. Future study may explore the application of other deep learning architectures and varied datasets to improve classification accuracy. Furthermore, combining additional imaging modalities beyond fundus images could expand the system’s application and improve diagnostic reliability.
