AB  - To address reliance on manual triage in current teleconsultation platforms, we propose a BTB intelligent triage model that supports next-generation 6G healthcare systems by integrating multi-feature fusion and Explainable AI (XAI) techniques. Assuming that the assigned consultation department in the dataset represents the optimal choice, the model uses patient age, gender, and preliminary diagnoses from primary care physicians to recommend appropriate specialty departments. To capture both local textual features and contextual dependencies in unstructured teleconsultation application records, the model combines BERT-based word embeddings, Text Convolutional Neural Network (TextCNN), and a double-layer Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (BiGRU) network. The extracted features are fused and passed through a fully connected layer to output a ranked list of recommended departments. The model was trained and validated on large-scale data from the National Telemedicine Center. When recommending the top five departments, it achieved an accuracy of 90.77% and a Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) of 76.45%. Moreover, the incorporation of explainability enables clinicians and patients to understand the rationale behind AI-driven recommendations, fostering trust and transparency. Experimental comparisons confirm that the proposed model significantly outperforms traditional machine learning approaches, offering a robust and interpretable solution for intelligent triage in teleconsultation-based consumer healthcare platforms.
