AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI)-powered medical chatbots are emerging as transformative tools in healthcare, offering capabilities that range from patient triage and diagnostic assistance to chronic disease management and medical education. Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs), convolutional neural networks (CNNs), and multimodal architectures have significantly enhanced their ability to process complex medical data and deliver contextually accurate, patient-specific responses. This paper critically examines 26 recent works, with a focused analysis of ten primary studies, to evaluate the methodologies, performance metrics, and clinical applicability of state-of-the-art medical chatbot systems. The review highlights models such as MiniGPT-Med, ECG-Chat, MediGPT, and CNN-LSTM hybrids, which achieved accuracies exceeding 90% in specific diagnostic tasks, while also identifying limitations related to dataset diversity, interpretability, and real-world validation. The discussion underscores key challenges, including regulatory compliance, privacy preservation, and integration into existing clinical workflows. By synthesizing cross-disciplinary insights, this paper provides a consolidated view of current progress, critical gaps, and strategic directions necessary to enable safe, ethical, and scalable deployment of AI-based medical chatbots in global healthcare systems.
