AB  - Dermatological diagnosis has faith in heavily on expert visual interpretation nevertheless remains vulnerable to variability arising from inconsistent imaging conditions, inadequate specialist availability, and subjective assessment. To incorporate these challenges, this paper introduces CogDermNet, a conceptual framework for real-time diagnostic support that incorporates cognitive science principles with artificial intelligence for detection of various types of skin disorders. The presented architecture incorporates cognitive visual awareness, memory-augmented reasoning, and multimodal contextual incorporation to mimic human perceptual and analytical activities. CogDermNet is aimed to process dermoscopic and clinical images acquired in various real-world sceneries and to produce interpretable predictions accompanied by human-associated justifications. Even though the system has not yet endured clinical or experimental estimation, this case study explains its potential applicability in primary care triage, remote dermatology, and telemedicine systems. By grounding AI components in cognitive procedures, CogDermNet seeks to enhance transparency, decrease diagnostic vagueness, and support trustworthy decision-making in forthcoming dermatology applications.
