AB  - Proposed Adaptive Multimodal Health Continuum Network (AMHC-Net) suggests an intelligent resource-based network that targets to offer trust and just medical services to underserved populations together with remote populations. The hierarchical structure of AMHC-Net is characterized by the synergistic mechanism demonstrating multi-modal sensing, adaptive edge computing, and distributed reasoning to offer continuous care provision that will persist under bandwidth and infrastructure limitations. The model combines the real-time data provided by physiological sensors, the environment and patient-provider interactions with transformer based multimodal fusion and graph attention networks respectively to infer contextualized health inference. Contingent on a Continuity Engine based on reinforcement learning, active adjustment of both communication and data prioritization is based on optimizing the maintenance of service quality under changing connectivity conditions. Furthermore, an explainable decision support layer acts as a bridge between the outputs generated by algorithms and the expertise of the clinician, for more interpretability and trust in algorithms toward automated diagnoses. The suggested system reduces the latency, streamlines the data flow, and provides the context-dependent triage, thereby tackling the discrepancy in the accessibility of care and accuracy of the diagnostic step. Access to 92% continuity efficiency with a more than 65 percent lower transmission load can be maintained in low-resource settings as shown by preliminary simulation analyses of AMHC-Net. AMHC-Net creates a paradigm shift in the future of reliable, adaptive, and inclusive digital health ecosystems, which can traversal barriers of infrastructures and socioeconomic systems and operate effectively. The suggested technique attains an overall accuracy of 94.8 percent, indicating exceptional reliability across all assessed measures.
