AB  - Childhood anemia is a critical public health problem in Peru, exacerbated by the limited availability of laboratory diagnostics in hospitals in rural areas. This paper describes the design and validation of a lightweight web platform that predicts the severity of childhood anemia based on clinical data collected during triage and blood hemoglobin levels. The underlying model is based on XGBoost and was trained on 30,000 clinical records following the guidelines of the Ministry of Health. Input variables include age, vital signs, symptoms, and hemoglobin. The model achieved an overall accuracy of 98.4% and an F1-macro of 0.984 with the full set of variables; it maintained 98.2% accuracy when using only ten key variables. The model was integrated into a web application accessible from any browser, and functional validation was performed with 50 real-world cases, achieving 97.9% clinical accuracy, an average latency of 1.4 seconds, and a SUS score (standardized usability scale) of 84.2. The results demonstrate that the solution is accurate, fast, and suitable for healthcare facilities with limited resources, offering a tool to support the timely diagnosis of pediatric anemia.
