AB  - Mobile health (mHealth) applications are increasingly incorporating artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance healthcare delivery on smartphones and tablets. This comprehensive survey examines AI-enabled mHealth across four domains: personalized health monitoring, diagnostic decision support, mental health coaching, and clinical triage. This research demonstrates that AI achieves significant improvements in predictive analytics accuracy (15–30% over traditional methods), personalized intervention effectiveness, and healthcare accessibility in underserved regions. Four detailed case studies reveal concrete impacts: an insulin dose calculator achieving 90% food recognition accuracy, skin cancer detection matching dermatologist-level performance (87% agreement), cough analysis tools detecting pediatric pneumonia with 87% accuracy, and AI triage chatbots providing clinically appropriate recommendations comparable to human physicians. Technical analysis contrasts on-device frameworks (TensorFlow Lite, Core ML) with cloud-based models, highlighting privacy–performance trade-offs. Critical challenges persist: 78% of users report privacy concerns, 23% of diagnostic systems exhibit algorithmic bias, and 68% app abandonment rates within 30 days, alongside evolving regulatory frameworks. This research identifies emerging solutions including federated learning, TinyML for edge computing, explainable AI for clinical trust, and large language model–powered conversational agents that address current limitations and enable next-generation intelligent mHealth systems.
