AB  - Kidney stone condition happens often and comes back again, so it is important to find stones early with Computed Tomography(CT) scans to prevent serious problems and help guide treatment.. This work proposes a CT-specific preprocessing pipeline and a feature-level ensemble model for slice-wise classification on a publicly available axial CT dataset. An image preprocessing and augmentation pipeline was used to enhance stone visibility and improve data quality before training the classification models. An ensemble model(ResNet18+DenseNet121) is constructed by concatenating 512- and 1,024-dimensional features and passing them through a multilayer perceptron fusion head. Trained with AdamW and early stopping (100 -epoch budget, best at epoch 23), the ensemble achieves a test accuracy of 0.9944, precision of 1.0000, recall of 0.9888, F1-score of 0.9944, Matthews correlation coefficient of 0.9889, and ROC-AUC of 0.9993, outperforming all single backbones. Three-fold crossvalidation yields a mean F1-score of 0.9663 with low variance, indicating robust generalization. Monte Carlo dropout was used to estimate how confident the model is in its predictions, allowing us to identify uncertain cases and support safer, more selective clinical decision-making, while LIME, SHAP, and Grad-CAM highlight clinically plausible stone regions. These results demonstrate that a carefully designed feature-level ensemble, combined with rigorous evaluation, uncertainty estimation, and explainable AI, can deliver reliable and interpretable support for CT-based kidney stone triage.
