AB  - Public holidays pose a major challenge for hospital emergency departments (EDs), as they combine reduced staff availability with uncertain demand. While forecasting models are widely used for hospital planning, most evaluations rely on longterm averages, which may obscure weaknesses during short, highimpact periods. This study forecasts ED admissions and hospitalizations during Easter Week and the Labour Day holiday of 2025 in Madrid, using machine learning and statistical models enriched with exogenous variables. A nationwide blackout on April 28th was specifically addressed to avoid bias. Results show that admissions are more predictable than hos-pitalizations, with shift-level aggregation substantially improving accuracy. Exogenous variables increased performance during Easter but had limited value for Labour Day. No single model was consistently superior, underscoring the importance of contextspecific approaches.
