AB  - Accurate and rapid identification of blood groups is critical in emergency medicine and healthcare delivery. Traditional serological methods are invasive, time-consuming, and require laboratory infrastructure. This study explores fingerprint images as a non-invasive biometric modality for blood group prediction using advanced deep learning architectures. The dataset of over 8,000 fingerprint images was preprocessed with augmentation, normalization, and noise reduction. Multiple models, including CNN variants, lightweight architectures, transformer-based models, and next-generation convolutional networks, were trained and evaluated. A weighted soft voting ensemble (0.5 EfficientNetV2-S + 0.5 MobileNetV3-Large) achieved the highest accuracy of 99.88%, outperforming individual models like MobileNetV3-Large (98.51%) and EfficientNetV2-S (97.79%). These results indicate that fingerprint-based deep learning provides a reliable, noninvasive approach for blood group prediction, with potential for deployment in mobile and point-of-care systems.
