AB  - A serious medical disorder known as intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) is defined by bleeding inside the skull and frequently calls for quick diagnosis and treatment. The usual imaging method for identifying ICH is non-contrast computed tomography (CT) scans because of their quick acquisition and excellent sensitivity to acute bleeding. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs), a type of deep learning technique, have been thoroughly investigated in recent years for the purpose of detecting ICH in CT scans. To leverage both spatial and sequential information in CT scans, hybrid models combining CNNs with recurrent layers like Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks have been proposed. These models process individual slices with CNNs and capture inter-slice dependencies using LSTMs, mimicking the radiologist's approach of reviewing consecutive slices. Such architectures have shown promise in enhancing detection accuracy. The study of Intracranial Hemorrhage (ICH) detection using deep learning has significantly advanced over the years, leveraging convolutional neural networks (CNNs), transformer-based models, and hybrid architectures to improve diagnostic accuracy in CT scans. Deep learning algorithms have demonstrated near-expert performance, surpassing traditional computer-aided detection methods in speed and precision. Techniques such as transfer learning, attention mechanisms (e.g., Vision Transformers), and multi-modal fusion have enhanced feature extraction, enabling better differentiation between hemorrhage subtypes. Despite challenges like data imbalance, interpretability, and generalization across diverse datasets, recent research has focused on federated learning, explainable AI (XAI), and domain adaptation to improve clinical applicability. Integrating AI-driven ICH detection into radiology workflows has shown promising results in reducing diagnostic delays, aiding emergency triage, and improving patient outcomes in neurocritical care settings.
