AB  - Cardiovascular diseases are closely associated with various psychological and physiological abnormalities, and their complex pathological mechanisms impose stringent requirements on diagnostic accuracy. In recent years, deep learning methods based on physiological signals have shown great potential for automated disease recognition and risk assessment. However, many existing methods rely predominantly on deep representations that often lack interpretability and fail to fully capture the intrinsic patterns embedded in physiological signals. To address these limitations, we propose a novel cardiovascular disease recognition framework that integrates multimodal feature fusion with self-supervised learning. The proposed method, termed ECG-MOEformer, jointly models 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) signals and clinical attributes to construct a comprehensive and interpretable disease representation. Furthermore, A contrastive self-supervised learning strategy is employed during pretraining to align cross-modal representations and enhance feature robustness using unlabeled data. Experiments conducted on the publicly available UK Biobank dataset demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves an accuracy of 0.94, a recall of 0.88, and an F1-score of 0.91, significantly outperforming several state-of-the-art baselines. Ablation studies further verify the effectiveness of the hierarchical temporal encoder and multimodal fusion strategy. These results indicate that ECG-MOEformer provides a robust and interpretable solution for intelligent cardiovascular disease recognition.
