AB  - Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is commonly diagnosed through clinical, biochemical, and ultrasound indicators, yet most AI studies isolate one modality. This work presents a unified, leakage-safe comparison between two pipelines: a soft-voting ensemble (XGBoost+LightGBM+CatBoost) trained on structured data and a ConvNeXt-Small model trained on high-resolution ultrasound images. Using public datasets (541 clinical records, 3,200 images), both are evaluated under identical 5-fold out-of-fold settings with explicit calibration. The clinical ensemble substantially outperforms the image model (AUROC 0.976 vs. 0.814; Brier 0.056 vs. 0.176). At stringent operating constraints (sensitivity/specificity ≥0.85), only the clinical model meets the target. Both achieve real-time inference (clinical: 57 ms, image: 30 ms), with the clinical ensemble offering 2.6× higher batch throughput. Decision-curve analysis and explainability tools show complementary insights. Results suggest structured features currently hold a stronger predictive signal, while ultrasound provides visual support and rapid triage capability.
