AB  - We study how AI-driven security automation can be systematically designed for resource-constrained cloud environments such as small business and medical environments in remote areas. Existing SIEM deployments often rely on loosely coupled integrations and manual triage workflows, lacking formalized closed-loop orchestration under latency and operational constraints. We propose a deployment-aware AI security orchestration framework that formalizes the trigger chain (event → triage → action → visualization → refinement) as a latency-aware closedloop workflow. Unlike conventional engineering integrations, our approach treats operability and inference latency as first-class design objectives. Our novelty lies in the formalization of an operationally constrained closed-loop orchestration and its measurable system-level metrics, rather than the specific vendor tools used The framework introduces three contributions. First, a system-level orchestration model that bridges AI inference with cloud deployment through explicit workflow formalization under operational constraints Second, a deployment-aware model training and selection strategy that structures event attributes into categorical, contextual, and behavioral feature groups and frames model choice as a multi-criteria decision problem balancing predictive accuracy, inference latency, computational overhead, and interpretability. Model outputs are mapped into operational risk tiers via a defined transformation function, enabling direct integration with automated response policies. Thirdly, a quantitative operational evaluation framework defining end-to-end workflow latency and reduction of manual intervention as measurable system metrics. The experiments on a large-scale labeled security event dataset demonstrate that the proposed framework reduces manual intervention and improves response efficiency while maintaining high classification precision. In this work refers both to the repeatable deployment of the cloud security pipeline and to the reproducibility of experimental results within the deployed environment. All components, datasets, and procedures are documented; all experimental artifacts are publicly released to ensure reproducible deployment and transparent validation. It provides a complete Project reproduction repository (including data, attack examples, experiment results, Analysis charts, dashboard demo), an available for local-run dashboard program code, and a steps operation manual for reproduce and verification. (Link: weijunma/AI-Driven-Cloud-Security-Framework-SMEHealthcare at V1.1.0 DOI 10.5281/zenodo.17605067).
