AB  - Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is frequently detected late, constraining therapeutic options. This paper develops an interpretable, data-integrated regression framework for estimating serum creatinine (SCr) from routinely collected clinical variables to support earlier assessment in laboratory-constrained settings. Two public datasets (UCI CKD and Kaggle CKD) were merged and harmonized; a hybrid imputation strategy handled missingness while preserving distributional properties across sources. The pipeline applied chi-square feature screening, standardized preprocessing, and hyperparameter tuning via 5-fold crossvalidation on the training partition $(80 \%)$, with performance reported on an untouched 20 % test set using mean squared error (MSE), root MSE (RMSE), and $R^{2}$. We compared a parsimonious linear baseline (OLS), two tree-based nonparametric regressors (Random Forest, XGBoost), and a probabilistic neural approach (Bayesian neural network with Monte Carlo dropout). On the held-out test data, the Random Forest regressor achieved the best fit ($\mathbf{M S E}=0.0158, \boldsymbol{R M S E}=0.126, \mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{2}}=0.98$), outperforming XGBoost ($\text{MSE}=0.0388, \text{RMSE}=0.197, R^{2}=0.95$) and the Bayesian neural network ($\text{MSE}=0.513, \text{RMSE}=0.716$, $R^{2}=0.38$); OLS underperformed ($\mathbf{M S E}=2.22, R^{2}=-2.67$). SHAP and partial-dependence analyses consistently highlighted hemoglobin, systolic blood pressure, fasting blood glucose, and age as dominant contributors, aligning with established clinical knowledge. By coupling high predictive fidelity with transparent, model-agnostic explanations, the proposed approach can enable pragmatic triage and longitudinal monitoring without expanding test panels. Future work will assess external validity on larger, prospective cohorts and examine transportability across care sites.
