AB  - We address report delays and priority mismatch during patient surges. We propose SA-UCA-CNN, a deployable, single-model framework for patient-level chest X-ray (CXR) triage. The method embeds a lightweight ViT-Adapter at each stage of a single CNN and fuses it with SAAF. This adds long-range and multi-scale representations with minimal overhead. During training, a Style-Consistency GAN performs crosshospital style alignment and long-tail augmentation. At inference, we keep only the CNN+Adapter path. We apply temperature scaling, selective prediction, and Monte-Carlo (MC) dropout. They map multi-label probabilities to three triage levels—urgent, high-priority, and routine—and provide calibrated uncertainty and Grad-CAM explanations. We train and internally validate on NIH ChestX-ray14. On CheXpert and MIMIC-CXR, the model reaches area under the receiver operating characteristic (AUROC)=0.900 and area under the precision-recall curve (AUPRC)=0.540, outperforming a pure CNN (0.820/0.410). The out-of-domain drop shrinks to $\triangle \text{AUROC}=-0.018$ with expected calibration error $(E C E)=0.036$. Inference latency is 22.5 ms. Ablations show that S3+S4 with medium-intensity style augmentation offers the best performance-cost trade-off. $S2 + S3 + S4$ performs slightly better but is slower. A GAN-only scheme supports ultra-low latency. An Adapter-only variant markedly improves calibration. Overall, the framework improves external generalization, long-tail recall, and risk consistency without adding deployment complexity. It provides an auditable and reproducible engineering path for clinical CXR triage.
