AB  - Introduction In recent years, the emerging technology of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has made huge strides in its application to medical diagnostics, achieving outstanding accuracy and efficiency. However, the sustainability of such applications has not been well-investigated. Such conventional deep learning models in healthcare require heavy computation, internet access and central available data storage, which raise an issue regarding energy consumption, fairness and sustainability. In this paper, we explore a green solution for triaging chest X-rays towards the detection of pneumonia using AI, by comparing a traditional cloud-based inference solution based on ResNet50 to a MobileNetV2 model compressed via federated learning and deployed on edge-devices. We then evaluated the two systems across diagnostic performance, energy consumption, demographic fairness, inference latency, and transparency on a subset of the NIH ChestX-ray14 dataset. The results suggest that the sustainable system retained a similar level of accuracy while significantly reduced inference energy (up to >80%), improving fairness measures and enabling explainability through SHAP-based visualization. Our results show that AI systems designed with sustainability in mind can deliver clinically effective and environmentally responsible systems, ideally suited to scalable and equitable deployment across diverse healthcare contexts.
