AB  - Medical image analysis is critical for early screening, triage, and treatment planning. However, automated methods face challenges due to small lesion sizes, heterogeneous acquisitions, and the trade-off between local spatial precision and global contextual reasoning. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) excel at capturing local textures but struggle with long-range dependencies, while vision transformers (ViTs) offer global context at the cost of boundary detail and computational efficiency. To address these challenges, we propose LGAF-Net (Local-Global Attention Fusion Network), a lightweight hybrid framework that integrates fine local cues with global anatomical context. The model employs a Swin-Tiny backbone for hierarchical feature extraction, applies self-attention at a 14×14 resolution for local refinement, and uses cross-attention from 7×7 tokens for global-to-local guidance. A gated fusion module adaptively combines these representations before classification, with Grad-CAM visualizations ensuring case-level interpretability. Evaluated on the Brain Tumor MRI dataset (four classes) and the Bone Fracture Multi-Region X-ray dataset (binary classification), LGAF-Net achieved 0.994 and 0.982 accuracy, respectively, surpassing CNN, transformer, and hybrid baselines by 3–14% while requiring only 27.5M parameters and 4.5 GFLOPs compared to ViT’s 86M and 17.6G. These results demonstrate LGAF-Net’s state-of-the-art accuracy and efficiency, offering a deployable and interpretable solution for clinical decision support.
