AB  - About 2.5 million people die from pneumonia each year, making it a serious worldwide health concern. The elderly and children under five are the most susceptible groups. Because of a lack of qualified radiologists and restricted access to medical facilities, around 60 % of pneumonia cases in low-resource areas are misdiagnosed or discovered too late. The primary diagnostic method is a chest X-ray, however manual interpretation is subjective and time-consuming, which causes therapy to be delayed. Deep learning approaches have become viable options for automated diagnosis as a result of the quick development of artificial intelligence in imaging medicine. In order to detect pneumonia and measure the severity of infection from an X-ray of the chest, we provide a deep learning-based system in this paper that integrates ResNet50 for classification and UNet for lung segmentation. Additionally, to improve interpretability for doctors, afflicted regions are highlighted using Grad-CAM visualization. High classification accuracy, strong segmentation performance, and accurate severity prediction are demonstrated in experiments on publically accessible chest X-ray datasets, allowing for quick and accurate evaluation. The proposed method is a broad and comprehensive artificial intelligence (AI), powered system that can assist healthcare providers in early diagnosis, triage, and treatment planning. Lower mortality rates and better patient outcomes may particularly be achieved in poor areas.
