AB  - Coronary Artery Disease is a progressive cardiovascular disease with the highest mortality rate. Therefore, it is crucial to diagnose this disease early for effective, individualized treatment. In this work, machine learning models are applied to disease diagnosis, starting with data preparation and preprocessing. In order to select features, the Hybrid In-Class selector is used, combined with tree-based relevance, class-conditional separation, and mutual information to rank variables within the training folds. With these selected features, a novel Calibrated Margin-Weighted Soft Voting Ensemble model is proposed, which initially calibrates the predicted probabilities from the base learners. It then dynamically assigns instance-specific weights based on the confidence margin, while incorporating stability constraints to ensure robust, reliable ensemble decisions. The experimental results are validated across two datasets, where the HIC selector achieved the highest accuracy on both D1 and D2, i.e., 93.3% and 92.3% with 10 features. The proposed model consistently outperformed other ensembles, achieving top results on D1 with F1 of 0.9237 and an accuracy of 0.92017, and on D2 with F1 of 0.91781 and an accuracy of 0.9278. Across other metrics, the proposed model also achieved superior scores, with further ROC and PR curves, and SHAP-based interpretability analysis was performed. The results of this work demonstrate accurate, recall-oriented coronary artery disease prediction suitable for screening and triage, without sacrificing specificity or efficiency.
