AB  - The integration of trustworthiness (including explainability, fairness, privacy, security, and reliability) into machine learning (ML) systems in healthcare must be integrated across all phases of the ML lifecycle. Currently however, most of the current approaches to these issues are post-hoc, fragmented, and addressed during different lifecycle phases after model training; therefore, they do not provide consistent or cohesive governance and evaluation of models. The objective of this paper was to propose a governance-first ML framework which incorporates trustworthiness constraints into each phase of the ML lifecycle. The proposed framework has a four layer architecture: Data Governance, Model Development, Deployment Controls, and Continuous Monitoring. In addition, the authors formulated model development as a constrained multi-objective optimization problem where predictive performance of models is aligned with compliance with governance requirements. Using six synthesized datasets that represent clinical and insurance based applications, the authors demonstrated the proposed method’s ability to produce models with significantly greater trustworthiness values (p¡.001) than five baseline methods (0.91 – 0.92 versus 0.48 – 0.75). Moreover, predictive performance was maintained at levels equivalent to those produced by the baseline methods (AUC = 0.89 – 0.91), thus demonstrating the feasibility of governance-embedded ML under controlled, synthetic evaluation conditions.
