AB  - Digital medical instruments are extensively applied to seek simple medical advice and enhance patient knowledge, particularly on the initial phase of symptom monitoring, over the last several years. Although medical chatbots do exist, despite this limitation, most of them use fixed rules or matching based on keywords, instead of the ability to interpret user queries within the context. The paper suggests a secure and explainable medical chatbot based on dialogue and supports adaptive symptom assessment through the transformer-based approaches of Natural Language Processing. The suggested system uses a fined-tuned BERT model to recognize intent among users and retrieve valuable clinical information about the symptoms, duration, severity and medication use in the free-form text input. To prevent partial comprehension, the chatbot will use an interactive line of inquiry, whereby more follow-up questions will be created whenever there is a lack of crucial clinical information. Such a style of interaction is quite similar to the behaviour of a simple medical triage process. In order to facilitate responsible usage, privacy-conserving algorithms are used to eliminate the personally identifiable information, and an explainability aspect is added to explain the way responses are generated. The Python and Flask implementation of the system mean it can be sustained with minimal resources and lightweight containerization, which are relevant to rural healthcare system settings, education portals, and telehealth systems. The system can be in the future enhanced with support of variety of languages, voice replies, and frequent subversion with new medical data.
