AB  - Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) is a leading cause of disability worldwide, affecting over 240 million people and placing a substantial burden on patients, carers, and healthcare systems. While recent Natural Language Processing (NLP) advances have enabled promising depression detection models, most focus on binary classification and cannot identify the specific symptom dimensions that guide clinical decisionmaking. This lack of symptom-level resolution limits their direct utility in personalised mental healthcare. To address this gap, we introduce SYMPTOM (SYMptom-level Prediction of Target Outcomes in MDD), a multi-class NLP pipeline designed to classify short mental health narratives into ten (10) Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5) symptom categories plus a recovery / healthy class. We fine-tuned the computationally efficient DistilBERT transformer and employed it on the purpose-built SYMPTOM dataset. This dataset is publicly available and consists of 6,800 expert-labelled, short-form narratives. It was created through participatory design with mental health professionals. The proposed model achieved an accuracy of 80.29 % and a weighted F1-score of 0.804, with top per-class performance for Appetite and Weight Changes $(\mathrm{F} 1=0.93)$, sleep disorders $(\mathrm{F} 1=0.90)$, and Recovery / Healthy $(\mathrm{F} 1=0.91)$. Even symptom categories with high semantic overlap, such as Suicidality & Self-harm (F1 = 0.81), were classified with strong accuracy. The mobile health readiness of the model stems from its low computational footprint: all experiments were conducted on a standard laptop equipped with a single consumer-grade GPU (NVIDIA RTX 3060, 6 GB VRAM) and 16 GB RAM, demonstrating that training and inference can be efficiently performed without high-performance infrastructure. By moving beyond binary detection toward clinically meaningful, symptom-specific classification, SYMPTOM bridges a critical gap between research-grade NLP models and actionable mental health tools. Its scalability, interpretability, and mobile health readiness make it particularly suited for deployment in resource-limited or remote settings, supporting early detection, triage, and personalized intervention in MDD.
