AB  - This study introduces a lightweight, deployable pipeline for automated retinal disease screening based on MobileNetV3-Large on color fundus images. Images were center-cropped to preserve the circular field of view and resized to 224 x 224, and mildly enhanced using green-channel CLAHE and normalized to [ -1,1]. The dataset was then divided into training (n = 1,577), validation (n = 339), and test (n = 338) subsets, and the labels were mapped to a binary outcome (Normal vs. Diseased). Fine-tuning a frozen backbone with a shallow head resulted in 98.5% test accuracy, with tightly balanced precision/recall and only 5 misclassifications: TN=176, FP=2, FN=3, TP=157. Learning curves evidence fast convergence without overfitting, confirming that MobileNetV3- Large is suitable for efficient ophthalmic screening. These results indicate robust generalization under simple, reproducible preprocessing that supports practical use in triage or tele-ophthalmology workflows. Further work will extend to multiclass retinopathy grading and macular-edema risk prediction, including calibration and explainability, and will be validated across multi-center cohorts to assess domain shift and clinical impact.
