TI  - Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Pharmacovigilance: Architectures, Applications, and Challenges for Digital Drug Safety Surveillance
AU  - S. Kharat
AU  - S. Mali
AU  - A. Ganachari
AU  - S. Gupta
AU  - T. Noor
T2  - 2026 Innovations in Machine, Engineering, and Digital Conference (IMED)
PY  - 2026
SP  - 1
EP  - 7
DO  - 10.1109/IMED68921.2026.11484148
PB  - IEEE
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - pharmacovigilance
KW  - signal detection
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Real-time ADR monitoring
AB  - Pharmacovigilance has expanded rapidly with the growth of real-world data, yet traditional approaches still face major limitations, including severe under-reporting (¿90%), fragmented data sources, and delayed signal detection, despite rising data volumes such as FAERS exceeding 20 million reports. The literature describes applications from case intake and data cleaning through signal detection, causality assessment, and triage of safety issues, using information from spontaneous reporting systems, electronic health records, social media, and other large safety datasets. Across these settings, AI tools are used to structure free text safety narratives, extract adverse drug reactions, and support near real time monitoring and automated alerting, with reported adverse reaction extraction accuracies typically in the 82–95% range and F measures around 0.72–0.82. Several groups also report substantial gains in efficiency, such as shorter case processing times or reduced false positive signals, and some Bayesian and network-based models appear to shorten formal causality assessment and enable more proactive risk forecasting. However, the performance of these systems still depends heavily on data quality, standardization, and bias, and important questions remain about legal and ethical governance, explainability, independent validation, and integration into routine pharmacovigilance infrastructure. Overall, the review suggests that AI should be treated as a set of decision support tools that complement, rather than replace, established pharmacovigilance practices, and that careful design, validation, and oversight will be essential if these methods are to improve drug safety and patient outcomes in real world settings.
