AB  - Using 78,000+ child records from ICDS Khunti (2023-2025) with age, sex, weight, height, MUAC, BMI, and WHO HAZ/WAZ/WHZ, we trained Random Forest and XGBoost classifiers for stunting, wasting, and underweight. Performance was consistently high (Random Forest F1 $=0.995- \mathbf{1. 0 0 0}$, ROC-AUC $\boldsymbol{\approx} \mathbf{1. 0 0}$; XGBoost $\mathbf{F 1} \boldsymbol{=} \mathbf{0. 9 9 3}-\mathbf{0. 9 9 9}$, ROC-AUC $\approx \mathbf{1. 0 0}$) under an 80:20 split and 5-fold cross-validation. Blocklevel prevalence was forecast to 2030 with ARIMA; one-stepahead 2025 tests yielded MAE/RMSE $=0.52-11.13$ and MAPE $=2.06-40.43$. Forecasts indicate persistent disparities: Karra has the highest stunting, surpassing $\sim 52 {\%}$ by 2030; Rania sustains the highest wasting (>11%); Arkee and Torpa remain highest for underweight $\boldsymbol{(} \boldsymbol{\sim} \mathbf{2 5} \boldsymbol{-} \mathbf{2 6 \%} \boldsymbol{)}$. Data were cleaned under WHO plausibility limits, missing WHZ imputed by median, and labels derived from WHO cut-offs. The combined child-level classification and block-level forecasting provide operational signals for ICDS/Poshan triage, targeting, and medium-term planning in high-burden blocks. SHAP analyses underscored MUAC, WHZ, and age as dominant predictors.
