AU  - B. Jonatha
AU  - F. F. Lubis
T2  - 2025 15th International Conference on Information & Communication Technology and System (ICTS)
PY  - 2025
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
SN  - 2996-1378
DO  - 10.1109/ICTS67612.2025.11369580
PB  - IEEE
KW  - Interpretable machine learning
KW  - emergency department
KW  - triage
KW  - electronic health records
KW  - systematic literature review
AB  - The triage system determines the severity of a patient's condition and the priority of emergency care. Conventional methods are still limited by the subjectivity of medical personnel, which leads to inconsistent decisions. Machine learning (ML) models improve the accuracy of clinical condition classification, although they still have limitations in terms of lack of readability, making prediction results difficult for medical personnel to understand. Interpretable machine learning (IML) approaches can overcome this problem without reducing prediction performance in decision-making. This systematic review analyzes 25 studies published between 2021 and 2025 in five major databases: Scopus, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, PubMed, and SpringerLink. The studies were analyzed based on their focus on research context analysis, dataset type, modeling methods, interpretability techniques, and reported final results. Two approaches dominated: gradient boosting models using SHAP or LIME, and score-based systems such as AutoScore and SERP. The reported prediction outcomes included hospital admissions, intensive care needs, mortality, risk of sepsis, and length of patient stay. Existing research gaps include a lack of multimodal methods, evaluation of fairness constraints, and a lack of data sources from low- and middle-income countries.
ER  - 

